-- Reading content: three passages with the full task spread for both exams.

INSERT INTO reading_passages (id, exam_version_id, title, subtitle, paragraphs, sources, word_count, difficulty, topic, tags, is_published) VALUES (
  $x$rp-container-01$x$, $x$ielts-2026-01$x$, $x$The Box That Shrank the World$x$, $x$How a steel container rearranged trade, ports and factories$x$, $x$[{"label": "A", "text": "For most of maritime history, cargo was carried loose. Sacks of coffee, barrels of oil, crates of machinery and bales of cotton were stowed piece by piece into a ship's hold by gangs of longshoremen, who fitted them together like an enormous and badly designed puzzle. A ship might spend longer tied up at a wharf than it spent at sea. In the early 1950s a vessel could sit in port for a week or more while several hundred men loaded and unloaded it, and roughly half the cost of moving goods by sea was incurred while the ship was standing still."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The man usually credited with ending this was not a shipping man at all. Malcolm McLean owned a trucking company in North Carolina, and his interest in ships began with his impatience at watching his drivers wait at the dockside. His insight was that the industry had been moving the wrong thing: it had been moving cargo, when it should have been moving containers. In 1956 he converted a surplus tanker, renamed the Ideal-X, to carry fifty-eight metal boxes from Newark to Houston. The boxes were lifted straight from truck chassis onto the deck."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The numbers explain everything that followed. Loading loose cargo onto a medium-sized ship cost around $5.86 a ton. Loading the same weight in containers cost about sixteen cents. Nothing else about the voyage had changed, the same ocean, the same fuel, roughly the same crew, but the expense of the part that happened in port had very nearly vanished. Cheapness of that order does not merely improve an industry; it rearranges everything that depends on it."}, {"label": "D", "text": "It did not happen quickly. The early years of containerisation were a period of expensive incompatibility, in which every operator built boxes to its own dimensions and each was useless to the others. Ports invested in cranes that could serve only one line's ships. Dockworkers' unions, correctly reading the technology as a threat to the number of men a wharf required, resisted it through a long sequence of disputes. Railways, which owned much of the waterfront land, were slow to see why they should cooperate."}, {"label": "E", "text": "What broke the deadlock was not a commercial victory but a committee. Between 1968 and 1970 the International Organization for Standardization settled on a common set of dimensions, principally the twenty-foot and forty-foot box, and, more importantly, on the design of the corner castings by which a container is gripped, stacked and locked. Once a box built anywhere could be lifted by a crane anywhere, the container ceased to be a company's equipment and became infrastructure."}, {"label": "F", "text": "The Vietnam War did more than any sales campaign to prove the point. The American military, struggling to supply a distant theatre through inadequate harbours, contracted McLean's Sea-Land to do it in containers, and the improvement was dramatic. It also handed him a commercial gift: his ships were returning from Asia empty, so he began calling at Japanese ports on the way home. The transpacific trade in manufactured goods, which would define the following half-century, effectively began as a way of filling empty boxes."}, {"label": "G", "text": "The map of world shipping was redrawn within two decades. A container port needs deep water, a great deal of flat land for stacking, and good road and rail links, requirements that the old finger piers of Manhattan and London's Docklands could not meet at any price. Cargo moved to Newark, to Oakland, to Felixstowe, to Rotterdam. Cities whose identity had been built on their waterfronts found that the water had, commercially speaking, gone somewhere else."}, {"label": "H", "text": "The ships themselves grew to match. The first purpose-built container vessels carried perhaps a thousand boxes. The Panamax generation, sized to the locks of the Panama Canal, carried around four thousand. Once operators accepted that some routes need not use the canal at all, the constraint disappeared, and the largest vessels now move more than twenty thousand. Each increase in size lowers the cost of carrying a single box and raises the cost of anything going wrong."}, {"label": "I", "text": "The wider consequence was that distance stopped being expensive. When moving a component across an ocean costs less than moving it across a country by road, a manufacturer can put each stage of production wherever it is cheapest, and the factory ceases to be a place and becomes a sequence of places. The supply chains that resulted are often described as a consequence of trade agreements or of cheap labour. They are at least as much a consequence of a steel box with reinforced corners."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Not everyone counts the result as a straightforward gain. The dockwork that supported whole neighbourhoods largely disappeared, and the ports that replaced it employ a fraction of the people, on land deliberately sited away from the city. Concentrating so much trade into so few deep-water harbours has also made the system brittle: a single vessel wedged across a canal, or a strike at one terminal, now propagates through economies that have no slack left in them. The box made the world cheaper to move around, and considerably harder to repair."}]$x$::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, 845, $x$medium$x$, $x$trade and technology$x$, $x${"trade","technology","history","globalisation"}$x$::text[], TRUE);

